{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793513529,"nonce":"keO5nAiMKiAK4O8+ZX+HLGG4N0g=","title":"M1: CIC terms and the lean4export reader/writer","message":"Parent epic: listed in a comment below. Depends on M0.\n\n\nC# data types for Lean's kernel terms, and a strict reader and writer for lean4export NDJSON, so Lean's already-elaborated libraries can be loaded and LeanSharp's own declarations written back out.\n\n- `src/LeanSharp.Cic`: `Name`, `Level`, `Expr` (12 kinds, cached hash and flags), `ConstantInfo`, `Declaration`, `Env`.\n- `src/LeanSharp.Lean4Export`: `Lean4ExportReader` (formats 3.0.0 and 3.1.0; 3.0.0 groups mutual definitions in arrays and uses different keys), `Lean4ExportWriter` (3.1.0).\n\n\n`Nat.add_succ`, init-medium and the arena files import; import → export → import yields identical constants; export is idempotent.\n\n\n- Commit `c1268aaeeeace548dc154fd0dd4f3c0b7288d266` \"Milestone 1: CIC terms and lean4export reader/writer\".\n- `tests/LeanSharp.Lean4Export.Tests/ReaderCorpusTests.cs` asserts record counts produced by an independent oracle (`scripts/count-export.py`), so the reader cannot merely agree with itself. init-medium: 5,118 constants in 4,631 declaration records.\n- `RoundTripTests.cs`: every constant survives import → export → import; export → import → export is byte-identical. `ReaderErrorTests.cs` covers malformed input.\n\nStatus: complete (design §11 marks it ✅). Tests were not re-run when this ticket was filed: `dotnet test` failed to start in the agent sandbox with a NuGet migration mutex error.","files":null}]}